@fotoole, the numbers you quote in the attached clip are fourteen years old. The 2021 census reversed the 2011 identity result outright (English-only 58% to 15%), and ONS puts most of that down to one thing. The state moved "British" to the top of the form and the count followed. "British" is the mask the English state applies to those it considers its subjects, to obscure Westminster's colonial actions. The 2021 form put the mask back on. A count that swings 43 points on the order of two boxes is measuring the form, not the people under it.

What never moved: no option Westminster offers anyone it counts as "British" exists outside Westminster. The English are the ones with no way round that. Sovereignty in England rests with the Crown-in-Parliament, not the people, and has since 1689. Westminster has never once been the parliament of the English people. Three-quarters said it should not make their laws and nothing happened, because there was nobody to tell. That is why the withdrawal you describe came out as Brexit. It had nowhere else to go.

So the practical point, from Scotland: the English withdrawing mentally changes nothing, because Westminster holds the exit and the English people cannot take it off them. The union ends when a people with a title to sovereignty exercises it. Ireland's route under the 1998 Act and Scotland's under section 30 both leave the exit in a Secretary of State's hand. Does reunification wait on that, or does Irish popular sovereignty end it? That is the ground we are fighting for bhon bhunait on behalf of Scots.

